#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186216959 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186217108 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "31b2bec9-3dff-557c-8cf5-a54f145fc45c" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the X-Coordinates of the Intersection Points of a Parabola and a Line"}] :question/id #uuid "44ed3aff-8a4b-520c-808f-9e3ff928ac74" :question/math-academy-id "e-19814" :question/problem "The parabola $y = x^2 + 2$ and the line $y = 3x$ intersect at the points $(x_1, y_1)$ and $(x_2, y_2),$ where $x_1 <        x_2.$ What is the value of $x_1 + x_2?$" :question/worked-solution "We need to solve the following system consisting of the equations of the curves:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}y={x}^{2}+2 \\\\ y=3x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting the equations equal to one another and solving, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+2 & =3x \\\\ {x}^{2}-3x+2 & =0 \\\\ (x-1)(x-2) & =0 \\\\ x & =1,2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the $x$-coordinates of the intersection points are $x_1 = 1$ and $x_2 = 2.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\nx_1 + x_2 = 1 + 2 = 3.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186216960 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186217109 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "797cb8e2-0230-56ec-8fda-7f2b40a2329c" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Y-Coordinates of the Intersection Points of a Parabola and a Line"}] :question/id #uuid "9aa14c81-aeaa-51b7-9d16-468e22bc113d" :question/math-academy-id "e-19815" :question/problem "The parabola ![](images/49/49d58a04c172fb017d3e63107af8686694a70f8b3b281b61f1bf80fbbce1903c.svg) and the line ![](images/da/dad6e7d0144b11544800cab8e15352011d03b14a429fd6b38518e6d764777fbb.svg) intersect at the points ![](images/58/586d275bc1ac46d094c469d88dfd59d0a63d2ede5496929e15923c42b180fd47.svg) and ![](images/af/afa2f1d8401bc59845387bc9c7d1d6b765abe9c638b3c28f1914f6bd9458e348.svg) where ![](images/ba/bac1330d96cc473aab0c7d4fb929105e307a215a8e6031084e7c9cc61cb87b66.svg) What is the value of ![](images/f5/f52303e047d6e35cf5061a15e4136e4f253bf4cdd2d421145f8a58b05389a469.svg)" :question/worked-solution "We need to solve the following system consisting of the equations of the curves:\n![](images/31/318d27072067aa70d893f2a1c0dc118ad7caced10b9ca5567b5bb0f91638ef30.svg)\n\nSetting the equations equal to one another and solving, we get\n![](images/c3/c3bb314de0a4b80fb6d51bbb155484aa968fee78d81011fd578cc8b8bca04df2.svg)\n\nSince ![](images/25/25273d659db203df5af353cdda1a390bdbb635b59363390a0ca00b45fdd24bb2.svg) the ![](images/fc/fcc5df999e3b49fae1685ee0ab5bd03709804e1dd49f2ee722c4269eb0f5506d.svg)-coordinates of the intersection points are ![](images/4f/4fe2b5629a57d8e984a53a0887f0ab10595a82fe303f8c70080cff9039ee200f.svg) and ![](images/42/428ebea63626be22a9e5b18f6f4bd8ceba019bd6d3e685a0621e843604f0f826.svg) \n\nTo find the ![](images/1d/1d9b846cface9f9348e4081a0dd87dd254bf26663c12aa0d1a15aa24d2393261.svg)-coordinates of the intersection points, we plug these values into either the curve or the line. Let's choose the line since it's simpler.\n\n- Substituting ![](images/e3/e3f9686f4fa57342e9e4415fe494e0361ef79005a9c92c2192d11f8da980481f.svg) we get\n![](images/6b/6bf49ff9ecea5536117e06be2a55625e2dd056609abec901f615ad74bd52c290.svg)\nso one point of intersection is ![](images/2f/2f7731330530547c0207184c710622d49d8b696419aaba6572ae0b65cc3672ee.svg)\n\n- Substituting ![](images/d7/d7657c785332cdb454794e479e9aae39f6d6f92532f10912f85c1bf3bc2fb317.svg) we get\n![](images/40/406ee9901d6670c3ddb3774fc575c5a1a80d33668dcb345a7a494f297df64147.svg)\nso the other point of intersection is ![](images/f0/f07ed4790b4cd3a20a4af1c48d680f6e6681feb972d35075171975f967eea468.svg)\n\nTherefore, ![](images/1d/1d7e290e5d4bac0018851250cc98d151231495c6cc9187aedaec060d1f9579bf.svg) and\n![](images/a5/a5c25be7330db098773c5566f2ce52d8f6cb65f06e0e9fc9bc4eb0b92a0ba534.svg)\n\nA sketch of the situation is shown below.\n\n![](images/da/da3cbc81f8170e3cf098a97d249e3ad8f0abf358fa641d8b94b1eb301f1a7858.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186216961 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186217110 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8cf321a6-6ef6-58a2-ab52-176a923fc086" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Intersection Point of a Parabola and a Line"}] :question/id #uuid "b24d4adc-8623-5fa9-9ac3-f95d7b4c0d1f" :question/math-academy-id "e-19816" :question/problem "The parabola $y=-x^2+6x$ and the line $y=9$ intersect at a single point with coordinates $(p,q).$ What is $p+q?$" :question/worked-solution "We need to solve the following system consisting of the equations of the curves:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}y=-{x}^{2}+6x \\\\ y=9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting the equations equal to one another and solving, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-{x}^{2}+6x & =9 \\\\ -{x}^{2}+6x-9 & =0 \\\\ -({x}^{2}-6x+9) & =0 \\\\ -(x-3)(x-3) & =0 \\\\ x & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the $x$-coordinate of the intersection point is $x=3.$ \n\nWe know the $y$-coordinate of the intersection point from the equation of the line. So, the only point of intersection is $(3, 9).$ Therefore, $p=3,\\; q=9,$ and\n\n$$\np+q = 3+9 = 12.\n$$\n\nA sketch of the situation is shown below.\n\n![](images/f9/f9d09e07988752af26143fa2dc8df260784be5fcb16c28d8115bb44fc3c1a4a1.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186216962 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186217111 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "76453c35-6f8e-5564-80f7-33fc0c8edbde" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Range of an Unknown Parameter Given That There Are No Intersection Points"}] :question/id #uuid "b2ead57b-8fd7-5d98-93fe-0e06290a5aa3" :question/math-academy-id "e-19817" :question/problem "Given that the line $y=x +2k$ does **not** intersect the parabola $y=x^2-x+3,$ what is the range of possible values of $k?$" :question/worked-solution "To determine whether the line $y=x +2k$ intersects the parabola $y=x^2-x+3,$ we need to solve the following system of equations for the two curves:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}y={x}^{2}-x+3 \\\\ y=x+2k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting the equations equal to one another and writing in standard form, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-x+3 & =x+2k \\\\ {x}^{2}-2x+3-2k & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve this equation using the quadratic formula with $a=1,\\, b=-2,$ and $c=3-2k{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{-(-2)\\pm \\sqrt{(-2)^{2}-4(1)(3-2k)}}{2(1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\pm \\sqrt{4-12+8k}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\pm \\sqrt{8k-8}}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the line and parabola do not intersect, our quadratic equation must have no real solutions; hence, the expression inside the square root must be negative.\nSetting up and solving this inequality, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}8k-8 & <0 \\\\ 8k & <8 \\\\ k & <1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the range of possible values of $k$ is $k \\lt   1.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186532682 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186217111 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "76453c35-6f8e-5564-80f7-33fc0c8edbde" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Range of an Unknown Parameter Given That There Are No Intersection Points"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6884fc73-9e1c-5600-b1f9-aaba1f71b390" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "k>6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532166} {:answer/id #uuid "7f22556c-3b2e-524d-85bb-92b3a3ca508b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "k<6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532220} {:answer/id #uuid "841b4151-23c6-5e4c-bcd4-1737cd56ac6b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "k<4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532231} {:answer/id #uuid "b208aace-0a45-5228-9c09-221962d12ff2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "k>-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532327} {:answer/id #uuid "e4b32aba-933a-56f5-8b1d-d0dea1394bd2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "k>24" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532422}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7f22556c-3b2e-524d-85bb-92b3a3ca508b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "k<6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532220} :answer-field/id #uuid "2bbfb83c-6d7a-5008-8767-3335022b3066" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532510}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "9485be5f-7e9e-52c2-8dc7-d3a495a119a9" :question/math-academy-id "q-152313" :question/problem "Given that the line $y=4x+k$ does **not** intersect the parabola $y={x}^{2}+6x+7,$ what is the range of possible values of $k?$" :question/worked-solution "To determine whether the line $y=4x+k$ intersects the parabola $y={x}^{2}+6x+7,$ we need to solve the following system of equations for the two curves:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}y={x}^{2}+6x+7 \\\\ y=4x+k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting the equations equal to one another and writing in standard form, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+6x+7 & =4x+k \\\\ {x}^{2}+2x+7-k & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve this equation using the quadratic formula with $a=1,\\,b=2,$ and $c=7-k:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{-2\\pm \\sqrt{{2}^{2}-4(1)(7-k)}}{2(1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{-2\\pm \\sqrt{4-28+4k}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{-2\\pm \\sqrt{-24+4k}}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the line and parabola do not intersect, our quadratic equation must have no real solutions; hence, the expression inside the square root must be negative.\nSetting up and solving this inequality, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-24+4k & <0 \\\\ 4k & <24 \\\\ k & <6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the range of possible values of $k$ is $k<6.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186532707 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186217108 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "31b2bec9-3dff-557c-8cf5-a54f145fc45c" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the X-Coordinates of the Intersection Points of a Parabola and a Line"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "283ebc04-f8a2-5328-b526-0807928a5a14" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532014} {:answer/id #uuid "d7787e12-314e-5739-b74c-e63de38ced70" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532394} {:answer/id #uuid "e5a9def2-c17c-5098-82fa-fe65f39f5c2f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532425} {:answer/id #uuid "e5ac17cb-7070-52ae-a020-36f3ef568515" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532426} {:answer/id #uuid "f0dd39fb-017e-5809-bd44-92c3cd0e399c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532453}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e5a9def2-c17c-5098-82fa-fe65f39f5c2f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532425} :answer-field/id #uuid "4c5c2381-aeb2-5e5f-8fa1-214d94fd8d00" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532528}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d109f13f-6c22-5d50-a9f4-da3fbec07962" :question/math-academy-id "q-155523" :question/problem "The parabola $y={x}^{2}-2$ and the line $y=x$ intersect at the points $({x}_{1},{y}_{1})$ and $({x}_{2},{y}_{2}),$ where ${x}_{1}<{x}_{2}.$ What is the value of ${x}_{1}+{x}_{2}?$" :question/worked-solution "We need to solve the following system consisting of the equations of the curves:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}y={x}^{2}-2 \\\\ y=x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting the equations equal to one another and solving, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-2 & =x \\\\ {x}^{2}-x-2 & =0 \\\\ (x+1)(x-2) & =0 \\\\ x & =-1,2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the $x$-coordinates of the intersection points are ${x}_{1}=-1$ and ${x}_{2}=2.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=-1+2=1.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186532663 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186217110 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8cf321a6-6ef6-58a2-ab52-176a923fc086" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Intersection Point of a Parabola and a Line"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "101089e6-8955-56e5-a9af-e1292704d66d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186531964} {:answer/id #uuid "32a2455a-2737-50ee-86bb-360681e30611" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532037} {:answer/id #uuid "85e130e6-d2bb-54dd-9b8e-4da5b5c088bb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532238} {:answer/id #uuid "a5c11f76-19e6-5543-8fb4-9888b01581c9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532307} {:answer/id #uuid "cd3a215d-79d6-530f-8102-b49ccd071024" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532374}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "85e130e6-d2bb-54dd-9b8e-4da5b5c088bb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532238} :answer-field/id #uuid "2d6ee34c-e06e-5482-9e88-a1329d3b48a1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532511}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "69684600-8c5e-5874-b78e-2cbd727505f9" :question/math-academy-id "q-155534" :question/problem "The parabola $y={x}^{2}+x$ and the line $y=3x-1$ intersect at a single point with coordinates $(p,q).$ What is $p+q?$" :question/worked-solution "We need to solve the following system consisting of the equations of the curves:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}y={x}^{2}+x \\\\ y=3x-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting the equations equal to one another and solving, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+x & =3x-1 \\\\ {x}^{2}+x-3x+1 & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}-2x+1 & =0 \\\\ (x-1)(x-1) & =0 \\\\ x & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the $x$-coordinate of the intersection point is $x=1.$ \n\nTo find the $y$-coordinate of the intersection point, we plug this value into either curve. \nLet's choose the equation of the line. Substituting $x=1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =3x-1 \\\\  & =3(1)-1 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the only point of intersection is $(1,2).$ Therefore, $p=1,\\,q=2,$ and \n\n$$\np+q=1+2=3.\n$$\n\nA sketch of the situation is shown below.\n\n![](images/53/53b8622b0d2fa3fa756d5520d24849fb633539fb471037b9fce6c97464a5fc20.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186532676 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186217108 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "31b2bec9-3dff-557c-8cf5-a54f145fc45c" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the X-Coordinates of the Intersection Points of a Parabola and a Line"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "904862d5-897c-534e-9e2f-e61c615561b1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532262}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "904862d5-897c-534e-9e2f-e61c615561b1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532262} :answer-field/id #uuid "abca62a1-f98f-5e60-8172-1b9e4959acc5" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532571} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4cef0f64-87f7-5719-93e3-ab9a559f0d4a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532093}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4cef0f64-87f7-5719-93e3-ab9a559f0d4a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532093} :answer-field/id #uuid "b8833578-9795-544f-95d8-425c9b507a3a" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532580}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "8a3fea1f-f2c7-5a54-bfc4-1db59932f81e" :question/math-academy-id "q-250072" :question/problem "The parabola $y=-{x}^{2}+3x+15$ and the line $y=4x-5$ intersect at the points $({x}_{1},{y}_{1})$ and $({x}_{2},{y}_{2}),$ where ${x}_{1}<{x}_{2}.$ Find the values of ${x}_{1}$ and  ${x}_{2}.$\n\n${x}_{1}=$\n{{field-1}}$,\\,{x}_{2}=$\n{{field-2}}" :question/worked-solution "We need to solve the following system consisting of the equations of the curves:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}y=-{x}^{2}+3x+15 \\\\ y=4x-5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting the equations equal to one another and solving, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-{x}^{2}+3x+15 & =4x-5 \\\\ -{x}^{2}-x+20 & =0 \\\\ -({x}^{2}+x-20) & =0 \\\\ -(x+5)(x-4) & =0 \\\\ x & =-5,4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $x$-coordinates of the intersection points are ${x}_{1}=-5$ and ${x}_{2}=4.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186532641 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186217109 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "797cb8e2-0230-56ec-8fda-7f2b40a2329c" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Y-Coordinates of the Intersection Points of a Parabola and a Line"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b1426c5a-cb34-58ac-9b8e-e7c4a43242b5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532324}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b1426c5a-cb34-58ac-9b8e-e7c4a43242b5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532324} :answer-field/id #uuid "065b0803-ec37-52f0-b035-55be85dd7364" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532493} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "19f7c4fa-35f3-5ea1-bf8d-567b0e9bd672" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "49" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186531987}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "19f7c4fa-35f3-5ea1-bf8d-567b0e9bd672" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "49" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186531987} :answer-field/id #uuid "5a8aefaa-4fc2-5f99-957b-9d1dc03b153e" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532532}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "3860fd8d-fa51-5495-8869-9f057f53d9a4" :question/math-academy-id "q-250073" :question/problem "The parabola $y={x}^{2}$ and the line $y=9x-14$ intersect at the points $({x}_{1},{y}_{1})$ and $({x}_{2},{y}_{2}),$ where ${x}_{1}<{x}_{2}.$ Find the values of ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}.$\n\n${y}_{1}=$\n{{field-1}}$,\\,{y}_{2}=$\n{{field-2}}" :question/worked-solution "We need to solve the following system consisting of the equations of the curves:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}y={x}^{2} \\\\ y=9x-14\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting the equations equal to one another and solving, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2} & =9x-14 \\\\ {x}^{2}-9x+14 & =0 \\\\ (x-2)(x-7) & =0 \\\\ x & =2,7.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince ${x}_{1}<{x}_{2},$ the $x$-coordinates of the intersection points are ${x}_{1}=2$ and ${x}_{2}=7.$\n\nTo find the $y$-coordinates of the intersection points, we plug these values into either curve. Let's choose the parabola since it's simpler.\n\n- Substituting ${x}_{1}=2,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} & ={x}_{1}^{2} \\\\  & =(2)^{2} \\\\  & =4,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso one point of intersection is $({x}_{1},{y}_{1})=(2,4).$\n\n- Substituting ${x}_{2}=7,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2} & ={x}_{2}^{2} \\\\  & =(7)^{2} \\\\  & =49,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso the other point of intersection is $({x}_{2},{y}_{2})=(7,49).$\n\nA sketch of the situation is shown below.\n\n![](images/4e/4e0ce399e32d7b957b71384d61cf08024b5d9995fde28d5f1fac71e1f123b2aa.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186532672 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186217109 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "797cb8e2-0230-56ec-8fda-7f2b40a2329c" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Y-Coordinates of the Intersection Points of a Parabola and a Line"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a59d5def-358e-53b7-8955-ecdd2b78feb6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532306}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a59d5def-358e-53b7-8955-ecdd2b78feb6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532306} :answer-field/id #uuid "75cc18fd-6c53-54eb-b993-7a84046752bf" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532546} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3b65d251-1335-5b26-a787-171e76d4951a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "32" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532057}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3b65d251-1335-5b26-a787-171e76d4951a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "32" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532057} :answer-field/id #uuid "eeeb0417-7377-5df8-aa31-302ace210d7c" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532605}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "82d663f5-9675-5779-8562-0b4d4248c2b2" :question/math-academy-id "q-250076" :question/problem "The parabola $y={x}^{2}+2x-3$ and the line $y=5x+7$ intersect at the points $({x}_{1},{y}_{1})$ and $({x}_{2},{y}_{2}),$ where ${x}_{1}<{x}_{2}.$ Find the values of ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}.$\n\n${y}_{1}=$\n{{field-1}}$,\\,{y}_{2}=$\n{{field-2}}" :question/worked-solution "We need to solve the following system consisting of the equations of the curves:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}y={x}^{2}+2x-3 \\\\ y=5x+7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting the equations equal to one another and solving, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+2x-3 & =5x+7 \\\\ {x}^{2}-3x-10 & =0 \\\\ (x+2)(x-5) & =0 \\\\ x & =-2,5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince ${x}_{1}<{x}_{2},$ the $x$-coordinates of the intersection points are ${x}_{1}=-2$ and ${x}_{2}=5.$\n\nTo find the $y$-coordinates of the intersection points, we plug these values into either curve. Let's choose the line since it's simpler.\n\n- Substituting ${x}_{1}=-2,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} & =5{x}_{1}+7 \\\\  & =5(-2)+7 \\\\  & =-3,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso one point of intersection is $({x}_{1},{y}_{1})=(-2,-3).$\n\n- Substituting ${x}_{2}=5,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2} & =5{x}_{2}+7 \\\\  & =5(5)+7 \\\\  & =32,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso the other point of intersection is $({x}_{2},{y}_{2})=(5,32).$\n\nA sketch of the situation is shown below.\n\n![](images/98/98d656438193d68da44573720da4a0fa5f7e1f7d253c29e0de2e309468c8b552.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186532696 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186217110 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8cf321a6-6ef6-58a2-ab52-176a923fc086" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Intersection Point of a Parabola and a Line"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "abedf875-4b66-5cf2-8796-0df622776e78" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532318}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "abedf875-4b66-5cf2-8796-0df622776e78" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532318} :answer-field/id #uuid "133c52bf-75f1-5b10-8319-1bbbc0724fa9" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532501} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a82dbd44-f08e-5a33-b1b6-6f9773885612" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532313}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a82dbd44-f08e-5a33-b1b6-6f9773885612" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532313} :answer-field/id #uuid "795ff076-e948-5018-81c9-39834569438a" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532549}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b39dbd28-21a4-5509-83b2-cf250304af27" :question/math-academy-id "q-250085" :question/problem "The parabola $y=-{x}^{2}-4x$ and the line $y=4$ intersect at the point $P.$ Find the coordinates of $P.$\n\nThe coordinates of $P$ are\n$(${{field-1}}$,$\n{{field-2}}$).$" :question/worked-solution "We need to solve the following system consisting of the equations of the curves:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}y=-{x}^{2}-4x \\\\ y=4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting the equations equal to one another and solving, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-{x}^{2}-4x & =4 \\\\ -{x}^{2}-4x-4 & =0 \\\\ -({x}^{2}+4x+4) & =0 \\\\ -(x+2)(x+2) & =0 \\\\ x & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the $x$-coordinate of the intersection point is $x=-2.$\n\nWe know the y-coordinate of the intersection point from the equation of the line. So, the only point of intersection is $(-2,4).$\n\nA sketch of the situation is shown below.\n\n![](images/18/187c98be201ec084cdd4fb21f0347f9d768c251cfedcacc9532d754741199e14.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186532710 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186217111 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "76453c35-6f8e-5564-80f7-33fc0c8edbde" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Range of an Unknown Parameter Given That There Are No Intersection Points"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "aae80146-7711-5756-a3e1-b69c4a8b8bcc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532317}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "aae80146-7711-5756-a3e1-b69c4a8b8bcc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532317} :answer-field/id #uuid "537c7c57-4db6-5792-bbce-5d1fd693dffa" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532531} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ec28ef51-9620-5155-9c04-545b698f6e12" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value ">" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532443}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ec28ef51-9620-5155-9c04-545b698f6e12" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value ">" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532443} :answer-field/id #uuid "bef92fe3-e86f-53cd-bf4b-51bb44134db0" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532582}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "dace6513-0d3a-5c62-b857-525a505a8ea4" :question/math-academy-id "q-250156" :question/problem "Given that the line $y=3x+2$ does **not** intersect the parabola $y={x}^{2}+5x+k,$ find the range of possible values of $k.$\n\n$k$\n{{field-1}}{{field-2}}" :question/worked-solution "To determine whether the line $y=3x+2$ intersects the parabola $y={x}^{2}+5x+k,$ we need to solve the following system of equations for the two curves:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}y={x}^{2}+5x+k \\\\ y=3x+2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting the equations equal to one another and writing in standard form, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+5x+k & =3x+2 \\\\ {x}^{2}+2x+(k-2) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve this equation using the quadratic formula with $a=1,$ $b=2,$ and $c=k-2:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{-2\\pm \\sqrt{(2)^{2}-4(1)(k-2)}}{2(1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{-2\\pm \\sqrt{4-4k+8}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{-2\\pm \\sqrt{12-4k}}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the line and parabola do not intersect, our quadratic equation must have no real solutions; hence, the expression inside the square root must be negative. Setting up and solving this inequality, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}12-4k & <0 \\\\ -4k & <-12 \\\\ k & >3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the range of possible values of $k$ is $k>3.$"}]}
